@@ -0,0 +1,114 @@
+# ADR-010: Validation gates run in CI, use SHACL core only, and amend rather than replace the ROBOT profile
+
+## Status
+
+Accepted (2026-09-10)
+
+## Context
+
+`docs/ADR.md` (ADR-005, ADR-009, ADR-010 there) describes a quality-assurance
+framework of ROBOT report, SHACL validation and SPARQL competency-question
+tests. On 2026-08-10 a read-only verification in the metasalmon hub (queue
+item B-44) found all three inert: the SHACL shapes had never been executed
+and, once executed, rejected the repo's own example data; the competency
+file held three queries in one `.rq`, which no engine parses; and
+`robot-profile.yaml` replaced ROBOT's default report profile with two INFO
+rules, so `--fail-on ERROR` had nothing it could fail on. Each had been green
+for months.
+
+Three choices had to be made to make the gates real, and each had a
+competing option worth recording.
+
+## Decision
+
+1. **SHACL runs under pyshacl, pinned, inside `make test`.** ROBOT 1.9.8
+   bundles Jena ARQ but not Jena SHACL (checked by listing the jar), so no
+   Java-only path existed. pyshacl is pinned to `0.31.0` in
+   `scripts/requirements.txt`, the newest release that resolves against the
+   `rdflib==7.2.1` pin CI already relies on for the bytes of
+   `docs/gcdfo.jsonld`; CI now installs from that file so there is one copy
+   of the pins. The alternative — keeping SHACL out of `make ci` and
+   documenting how to run it by hand — is what the repo had, in effect, and
+   it is a gate nothing runs.
+
+2. **The shapes use SHACL core only.** The two method-specific shapes need
+   conditional targeting (constraints that apply only to snorkel surveys, or
+   only to aerial ones). A SHACL-AF SPARQL-based target (`sh:target [ a
+   sh:SPARQLTarget ]`) expresses that directly and keeps per-property
+   messages, and pyshacl supports it under `--advanced`. It was rejected
+   because an engine that does not implement SHACL-AF, or a pyshacl run
+   without `--advanced`, ignores such a target **silently** and reports a
+   vacuous pass — measured: the same shapes, same data, `Conforms: True`
+   without the flag and `Conforms: False` with it. A silently vacuous shape
+   is the defect being fixed. The core idiom used instead is an implication:
+   `sh:or ( [ not this method ] [ this method's constraints ] )`. Its cost is
+   a coarser report — engines report an `sh:or` failure at the shape, not
+   the property — mitigated by a shape-level `sh:message` that names every
+   constraint the branch holds.
+
+3. **`robot-profile.yaml` carries the whole default profile with two rules
+   changed, and a test pins that.** ROBOT offers no way to amend the default
+   profile; `--profile` replaces it. So the file is `report_profile.txt` from
+   the pinned jar with `missing_label` and `missing_definition` at INFO, and
+   `scripts/validation-selftest.sh` fails if it differs from the jar's
+   default by anything else — including after a ROBOT upgrade, which is the
+   moment the file must be re-derived. The alternative of listing only the
+   overrides is not available in ROBOT, and the alternative of a longer
+   hand-curated list is how the two-line file came to be.
+
+4. **Every gate has a fixture that makes it fail, and the self-test runs in
+   `make test`.** `tests/fixtures/{shacl,sparql,robot}/` hold data broken on
+   purpose; the self-test asserts each gate fails on its fixture *for the
+   documented reason* (message present, control node absent, exact ERROR
+   rule), not merely that it exits non-zero. The self-test was shown to go
+   red against the pre-fix shapes and the pre-fix profile before it was
+   wired in.
+
+5. **A zero-row competency query is reported, not failed — for now.** All
+   three shipped queries return zero rows because they name terms the
+   ontology does not declare. Failing on zero rows today would fail CI on a
+   term decision this change does not make; hiding the count would be the
+   old vacuity in a new place. The runner prints the count, and
+   `docs/tech-debt.md` records the retirement condition under which zero
+   rows becomes a failure.
+
+## Consequences
+
+### Positive
+
+- `make ci` now executes the shapes, the queries and all 32 default ROBOT
+  rules, and fails if any of the three gates stops being able to fail.
+- The first run of the re-enabled ROBOT checks found a genuine defect
+  (`duplicate_label` on `gcdfo:WildSalmonPolicy` / `gcdfo:WildSalmonPolicyTheme`).
+
+### Negative
+
+- `make ci` is red on `main` until that duplicate label is resolved — by a
+  label change, which is a term decision, not by editing the profile.
+- One Python gate in an otherwise Java toolchain; contributors need
+  `pip install -r scripts/requirements.txt`.
+- SHACL reports for the conditional shapes name the shape, not the failing
+  property.
+
+### Neutral
+
+- `ontology/sparql/example-competency-questions.rq` is gone; its three
+  queries live one per file under `ontology/sparql/competency/`, unchanged
+  in what they ask.
+
+## More Information
+
+- The verification that found the gates inert:
+  metasalmon `knowledge/plans/2026-08-10-gcdfo-validation-layer-verification.md`.
+- The before/after evidence for each gate is in the pull request that landed
+  this ADR and in `CHANGELOG.md` (Unreleased).
+- `docs/ROBOT_SETUP.md` carries the profile's provenance and the current
+  baseline; `docs/tech-debt.md` carries what the running gates found.
+
+## Related
+
+- [ADR-007](007-webvowl-duplicate-node-disambiguation.md) — the previous
+  placebo gate in this repo, and the lesson that a gate needs a test it can
+  fail.
+- `docs/ADR.md` ADR-005, ADR-009, ADR-010 — the framework these gates
+  implement.
